#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kitty-fullscreen-guard — the last gate before anything empties a kitty OS window.

WHY THIS EXISTS (docs/research/kitty-crash-2026-10-15/README.md § Chosen fix, lines 337-391)
    Destroying the last pane of a native-fullscreen kitty window closes the NSWindow while AppKit
    still holds it in fullscreen — the exact state of the 2026-10-15 crash stack
    (`_NSExitFullScreenTransitionController dealloc` on an already-closed window). Every destroy
    path behaves the same (move, close, child exit), so every caller runs this guard immediately
    before its destructive call, and acts only on exit 0.

USAGE
    kitty-fullscreen-guard --window <kitty window id> [--to <address>] --mode toggle|refuse
                           [--scope pane|tab|os-window] [--tool <caller name>]
                           [--then <kitty action> [action args...]]      # must be the LAST option
    kitty-fullscreen-guard --any-fs --kitty-pid <pid>

EXIT CODES
    0  safe to empty the window NOW (or the removal would not empty the OS window at all — for
       --scope pane, counting sibling closes already told safe in the last 30 s; § CLAIMS)
    1  only with --then: the guard said safe but the follow-up action failed
       (with --then, 3 also covers focus moving off the window during the wait, and 4 the window
       vanishing — in both cases nothing was done)
    2  usage
    3  refused — the caller must not act
    4  the window was not found — the caller must not act either

HOW IT DECIDES
    1. A no-UI kitten (lib/kitty-fs-kitten.py, `kitty @ kitten --match id:<wid>`) reports whether
       removing --scope empties the OS window, and its platform_window_id. Not last → exit 0 with
       no state read (for --scope pane, only once the claim gate below agrees).
    2. The state is read OUTSIDE kitty, from the CGS Space types of that platform window (the same
       SPI as docs/research/kitty-crash-2026-10-15/repro/probe.swift:18-50): FS = the types include
       4; WINDOWED = exactly [0] and onscreen; UNKNOWN = anything else. The Space type is not a
       transition detector (it leaves 4 up to 0.39 s before an exit animation ends and never reads
       4 during an enter), so "windowed" only counts after it has held for STABLE_S, longer than the
       longest transition measured (0.90 s). One UNKNOWN shape is a window at rest: exactly [0],
       bounds present, NOT onscreen — a normal window on a desktop the display is not showing.
       Before any toggle it counts as safe (safe-windowed-offscreen) after BG_STABLE_S of identical
       windowed reads; any other read in that hold refuses. After a toggle it stays UNKNOWN.
    3. --mode refuse (autonomous callers) never toggles. --mode toggle (operator-initiated) toggles
       an FS window out of fullscreen ONCE — only when it is onscreen, so the operator is never
       pulled to a Space they were not looking at — then waits for a stable windowed read, and never
       toggles again. The exit itself DOES move the operator's view: macOS takes the window, and
       the display, to the regular desktop (docs/research/kitty-lastpane-move-2026-10-19/README.md).

RELAX ON THE FIX (2026-10-16; README § Corrections after upstream review, and the follow-up brief's
corrections F1-F4, F9 in ~/.claude/autonomy/incidents/kitty-crash-2026-10-15/upstream-2026-10-16/)
    A kitty running our fullscreen-destroy patch WITH the upstream backport leaves fullscreen itself
    before it closes, so refusing a fullscreen last pane the operator is looking at protects nothing
    there. The kitten reports `pid` and `fsfix` (the ObjC class GLFWFsFixV2 is registered in that
    process; only the rebuilt bundle carries it — GLFWDeferredClose alone is NOT enough, F2). One
    call is RELAXED iff all three hold:
      · the policy below is not `enforce`;
      · the running kitty reports fsfix true (relaxed="fsfix"), or the policy relaxes stock kitty
        for this mode (relaxed="policy", D1 options B/C);
      · the verdict is refused-fs and the last read was FS with onscreen=True.
    A fullscreen window that is NOT onscreen keeps rc 3: its exit transition would run on a Space the
    operator is not looking at, which was never measured (README § Residuals, "whether macOS
    switches the operator to that Space"; F1). fsfix false or None (unreadable) means not fixed.
    A relaxable call is read-only — toggle mode is coerced to refuse mode: it never sends a toggle,
    and an UNKNOWN read refuses at once rather than getting toggle mode's UNKNOWN_DEADLINE_S grace —
    and its rc 3 becomes 0: the verdict row keeps its verdict and adds would_rc=3,
    relaxed and enforced=false; there is no stderr refusal and no alert, and --then still runs.
    rc 2 and rc 4 never change. Every verdict row also carries the fsfix and policy it was decided on.

    --any-fs runs when remote control is DOWN, so it cannot ask the kitten. It reads the last non-None
    fsfix this guard cached for that kitty process at <state dir>/fsfix-<pid>-<start epoch>, keyed on
    pid AND process start time (`ps -o lstart=`, as scripts/kitty-build-swap.sh cmd_verify reads
    it), so a stock kitty that reuses a patched kitty's pid after a rollback never inherits "true"
    (F4). Missing, unreadable or another start's cache means not fixed. It cannot tell which window
    the pane lives in, so it relaxes only when EVERY fullscreen window of that kitty reads onscreen
    (and no read failed): whichever window it is, it is then one the operator is looking at.

POLICY — ONE FILE, ONE LINE (operator decision D1, cc-decide e3920969aba9)
    <state dir>/policy, where <state dir> = $CC_KITTY_FS_GUARD_STATE_DIR or
    ~/.claude/state/kitty-fs-guard. A file and not an env var: the chords and every kitty-spawned
    pane inherit KITTY's environment, which no shell export reaches (F9). Its first non-blank,
    non-# line selects:
      absent, empty or block-stock  D1 option A, the default: stock kitty enforces as before; relax
                                    only on fsfix, as above
      enforce                       never relax, not even on the patched build
      relax-stock-refuse            D1 option B: ALSO relax on stock kitty, refuse mode and --any-fs
                                    only (operator chords keep leaving fullscreen first)
      relax-stock                   D1 option C: ALSO relax on stock kitty in both modes; toggle mode
                                    then never toggles
      anything else, or unreadable  treated as enforce, said once on stderr (fail closed)
    The onscreen requirement holds under every policy. Flipping D1 is one line:
        mkdir -p ~/.claude/state/kitty-fs-guard && echo relax-stock-refuse >~/.claude/state/kitty-fs-guard/policy
    (or relax-stock / enforce), and `rm -f ~/.claude/state/kitty-fs-guard/policy` restores the default.
    --mode toggle stays a valid flag under every policy, so no caller's argv changes.

CLAIMS — CONCURRENT "NOT LAST" ANSWERS (docs/research/husk-history-2026-10-24/FIX-DESIGN.md D7)
    Two panes of one OS window closing together each see "2 panes, not last" and the window empties.
    So for --scope pane the kitten also returns `pane_wids` (one window id per window group, as it
    counts panes) and `pane_wid` (the group of --window). Under flock on <claims dir>/claims-<kitty
    pid>.json (claims dir = $CC_KITTY_FS_GUARD_CLAIMS_DIR or the state dir), remaining = pane_wids
    − self − other wids claimed within CLAIM_TTL_S (30 s). remaining ≥ 1 ⇒ safe-not-last, and only
    then this pane's claim {wid: ts} is written; remaining = 0 ⇒ the last-pane path (step 2).
    Counting against the snapshot's identities stays sound for stale snapshots and joins: a gone
    pane is not listed, a leaving one is excluded until its claim ages out. There is no count-based
    release. A reply without pane_wids, or one whose length differs from panes_in_os_window, is
    claim "unavailable"; an OSError on the claims file is claim "error" — both keep the HEAD answer
    (safe-not-last). The verdict row records `claim` (claimed|last|unavailable|error|n/a) and
    `pending` (other live claims on this snapshot's panes). Cost, failing closed: a claim from a
    call whose caller then did not close blocks a sibling's close for ≤30 s.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import fcntl
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from typing import Any, NamedTuple

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "..", "lib"))
try:
    from kitty_pane_actions import log_row
except Exception:

    def log_row(*a: Any, **k: Any) -> None:
        pass


TOOL = "kitty-fullscreen-guard"
SAMPLE_S = 0.1  # poll interval
STABLE_S = 1.0  # a windowed read counts only after holding this long (> 0.90 s longest transition)
BG_STABLE_S = 2.0  # a windowed read on a desktop NOT on screen: > 2x the longest transition (0.90 s)
UNKNOWN_DEADLINE_S = 3.0  # toggle mode, before toggling: UNKNOWN is polled this long, then refused
AFTER_DEADLINE_S = 8.0  # after the one toggle: stable windowed must arrive by then
KITTY_CALL_TIMEOUT_S = 5.0
ALERT_TIMEOUT_S = 60.0
CLAIM_TTL_S = 30.0  # a "not last" claim excludes its pane from siblings' counts this long

FS, WINDOWED, UNKNOWN = "FS", "WINDOWED", "UNKNOWN"
SCOPES = ("pane", "tab", "os-window")
# The policy file's values (docstring § POLICY). BLOCK_STOCK is D1 option A, the default.
BLOCK_STOCK, ENFORCE, RELAX_STOCK_REFUSE, RELAX_STOCK = (
    "block-stock",
    "enforce",
    "relax-stock-refuse",
    "relax-stock",
)
POLICIES = (BLOCK_STOCK, ENFORCE, RELAX_STOCK_REFUSE, RELAX_STOCK)
KITTEN = os.path.realpath(
    os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "..", "lib", "kitty-fs-kitten.py")
)


class WindowState(NamedTuple):
    state: str
    types: list[int] | None
    onscreen: bool | None
    bounds: tuple[float, float, float, float] | None


class NotFound(Exception):
    pass


class KittenFailed(Exception):
    pass


# ── the control-socket address ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Copied VERBATIM from bin/kitty-confirm-close (its comment block explains the duplication; this is
# the fourth copy). Used only when --to and KITTY_LISTEN_ON are both absent — a
# `launch --type=background` child (the native-binding rebinds in config/kitty.conf) has neither.
# tests/kitty-socket-address.bats holds the copies' constants in agreement.
KITTY_PID = "{kitty_pid}"
DEFAULT_LISTEN_ON = "unix:/tmp/kitty-{kitty_pid}"
_LISTEN_ON = re.compile(r"[ \t]*listen_on[ \t]+([^#]*)")


def kitty_conf() -> str:
    return os.environ.get("CC_KITTY_CONF") or os.path.expanduser("~/.config/kitty/kitty.conf")


def listen_on_template() -> str:
    tmpl = ""
    try:
        with open(kitty_conf(), encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                m = _LISTEN_ON.match(line)
                if m:
                    value = "".join(m.group(1).split())
                    if value:
                        tmpl = value
    except OSError:
        pass
    return tmpl or DEFAULT_LISTEN_ON


def _socket(path: str) -> str | None:
    try:
        if stat.S_ISSOCK(os.stat(path).st_mode):
            return f"unix:{path}"
    except OSError:
        pass
    return None


def socket_address() -> str | None:
    env = os.environ.get("KITTY_LISTEN_ON", "")
    if env and not env.startswith("fd:"):
        return env

    tmpl = listen_on_template()
    if not tmpl.startswith("unix:/"):
        return tmpl

    path = tmpl[len("unix:") :]
    found = _socket(path.replace(KITTY_PID, str(os.getppid())))
    if found:
        return found

    if KITTY_PID not in path:
        return None

    shape = re.compile(re.escape(path).replace(re.escape(KITTY_PID), r"\d+"))
    addrs = [
        addr
        for addr in (
            _socket(p)
            for p in sorted(glob.glob(path.replace(KITTY_PID, "*")))
            if shape.fullmatch(p)
        )
        if addr
    ]
    return addrs[0] if len(addrs) == 1 else None


# ── seams: the three things tests replace in-process ─────────────────────────────────────────────


def _now() -> float:
    return time.monotonic()


def _sleep(seconds: float) -> None:
    time.sleep(seconds)


def _wall() -> float:
    # Claims are shared between guard PROCESSES, so their age is wall-clock time, not monotonic.
    return time.time()


def kitty_bin() -> str:
    return (
        os.environ.get("CC_TERM_KITTY")
        or shutil.which("kitty")
        or "/Applications/kitty.app/Contents/MacOS/kitty"
    )


def _at(addr: str | None) -> list[str]:
    return [kitty_bin(), "@"] + (["--to", addr] if addr else [])


def kitten_query(addr: str | None, wid: int, toggle: bool = False) -> dict[str, Any]:
    """Ask the kitten about <wid> (and toggle its OS window's fullscreen when asked).

    Raises NotFound for a MatchError (kitty's text: `No matching windows for expression: id:N`,
    rc/base.py:46) and KittenFailed for anything else that is not one JSON object about <wid>.
    """
    cmd = _at(addr) + ["kitten", "--match", f"id:{wid}", KITTEN, "toggle" if toggle else "query"]
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=KITTY_CALL_TIMEOUT_S)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        raise KittenFailed(f"kitty @ kitten: {e}") from e
    if r.returncode != 0:
        if "No matching windows" in (r.stderr or "") + (r.stdout or ""):
            raise NotFound((r.stderr or "").strip())
        raise KittenFailed(f"kitty @ kitten rc={r.returncode}: {(r.stderr or '').strip()[:200]}")
    try:
        info = json.loads(r.stdout)
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, TypeError) as e:
        raise KittenFailed(f"kitten reply is not JSON: {(r.stdout or '')[:200]!r}") from e
    if not isinstance(info, dict) or "error" in info or info.get("wid") != wid:
        raise KittenFailed(f"kitten reply unusable: {info!r}"[:300])
    return info


# ── the CGS state read (ctypes, outside kitty) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

_CG_PATH = "/System/Library/Frameworks/CoreGraphics.framework/CoreGraphics"
_CF_PATH = "/System/Library/Frameworks/CoreFoundation.framework/CoreFoundation"
_kCFNumberSInt64Type = 4
_kCGWindowListOptionAll = 0
_kCGWindowListOptionIncludingWindow = 1 << 3
_SPACE_MASK_ALL = 0x7
_cgs_cache: Any = None


def _cgs() -> Any:
    """dlopen CoreGraphics + CoreFoundation once and type every call. Raises on any load failure."""
    global _cgs_cache
    if _cgs_cache is not None:
        return _cgs_cache
    import ctypes as c

    cg, cf = c.CDLL(_CG_PATH), c.CDLL(_CF_PATH)
    try:
        conn_fn = cg._CGSDefaultConnection
    except AttributeError:
        conn_fn = cg.CGSMainConnectionID
    conn_fn.restype, conn_fn.argtypes = c.c_int32, []
    cg.CGSCopySpacesForWindows.restype = c.c_void_p
    cg.CGSCopySpacesForWindows.argtypes = [c.c_int32, c.c_int32, c.c_void_p]
    cg.CGSSpaceGetType.restype = c.c_int32
    cg.CGSSpaceGetType.argtypes = [c.c_int32, c.c_uint64]
    cg.CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo.restype = c.c_void_p
    cg.CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo.argtypes = [c.c_uint32, c.c_uint32]

    class CGRect(c.Structure):
        _fields_ = [("x", c.c_double), ("y", c.c_double), ("w", c.c_double), ("h", c.c_double)]

    cg.CGRectMakeWithDictionaryRepresentation.restype = c.c_bool
    cg.CGRectMakeWithDictionaryRepresentation.argtypes = [c.c_void_p, c.POINTER(CGRect)]
    cf.CFNumberCreate.restype = c.c_void_p
    cf.CFNumberCreate.argtypes = [c.c_void_p, c.c_long, c.c_void_p]
    cf.CFNumberGetValue.restype = c.c_bool
    cf.CFNumberGetValue.argtypes = [c.c_void_p, c.c_long, c.c_void_p]
    cf.CFArrayCreate.restype = c.c_void_p
    cf.CFArrayCreate.argtypes = [c.c_void_p, c.POINTER(c.c_void_p), c.c_long, c.c_void_p]
    cf.CFArrayGetCount.restype = c.c_long
    cf.CFArrayGetCount.argtypes = [c.c_void_p]
    cf.CFArrayGetValueAtIndex.restype = c.c_void_p
    cf.CFArrayGetValueAtIndex.argtypes = [c.c_void_p, c.c_long]
    cf.CFDictionaryGetValue.restype = c.c_void_p
    cf.CFDictionaryGetValue.argtypes = [c.c_void_p, c.c_void_p]
    cf.CFBooleanGetValue.restype = c.c_bool
    cf.CFBooleanGetValue.argtypes = [c.c_void_p]
    cf.CFRelease.restype, cf.CFRelease.argtypes = None, [c.c_void_p]

    def key(name: str) -> int:
        return c.c_void_p.in_dll(cg, name).value

    _cgs_cache = {
        "c": c,
        "cg": cg,
        "cf": cf,
        "conn": conn_fn(),
        "CGRect": CGRect,
        "callbacks": c.addressof(c.c_char.in_dll(cf, "kCFTypeArrayCallBacks")),
        "kOnscreen": key("kCGWindowIsOnscreen"),
        "kBounds": key("kCGWindowBounds"),
        "kNumber": key("kCGWindowNumber"),
        "kPid": key("kCGWindowOwnerPID"),
        "kLayer": key("kCGWindowLayer"),
    }
    return _cgs_cache


def _cf_int(L: Any, num: int | None) -> int | None:
    if not num:
        return None
    c = L["c"]
    out = c.c_int64(0)
    return out.value if L["cf"].CFNumberGetValue(num, _kCFNumberSInt64Type, c.byref(out)) else None


def _space_types(L: Any, pwid: int) -> list[int] | None:
    """CGSCopySpacesForWindows(conn, 0x7, [pwid]) → each Space's CGSSpaceGetType. None = read failed."""
    c, cf, cg = L["c"], L["cf"], L["cg"]
    val = c.c_int64(pwid)
    num = cf.CFNumberCreate(None, _kCFNumberSInt64Type, c.byref(val))
    if not num:
        return None
    try:
        vals = (c.c_void_p * 1)(num)
        arr = cf.CFArrayCreate(None, vals, 1, L["callbacks"])
        if not arr:
            return None
        try:
            spaces = cg.CGSCopySpacesForWindows(L["conn"], _SPACE_MASK_ALL, arr)
        finally:
            cf.CFRelease(arr)
    finally:
        cf.CFRelease(num)
    if not spaces:
        return None
    try:
        types = []
        for i in range(cf.CFArrayGetCount(spaces)):
            sid = _cf_int(L, cf.CFArrayGetValueAtIndex(spaces, i))
            if sid is None:
                return None
            types.append(int(cg.CGSSpaceGetType(L["conn"], sid)))
        return types
    finally:
        cf.CFRelease(spaces)


def _row_facts(L: Any, row: int) -> tuple[bool, tuple[float, float, float, float] | None]:
    c, cf, cg = L["c"], L["cf"], L["cg"]
    on = cf.CFDictionaryGetValue(row, L["kOnscreen"])  # absent when offscreen
    onscreen = bool(on) and bool(cf.CFBooleanGetValue(on))
    bounds = None
    bd = cf.CFDictionaryGetValue(row, L["kBounds"])
    if bd:
        rect = L["CGRect"]()
        if cg.CGRectMakeWithDictionaryRepresentation(bd, c.byref(rect)):
            bounds = (rect.x, rect.y, rect.w, rect.h)
    return onscreen, bounds


def classify(types: list[int] | None, onscreen: bool | None) -> str:
    if types and 4 in types:
        return FS
    if types == [0] and onscreen:
        return WINDOWED
    return UNKNOWN


def read_window_state(platform_window_id: Any) -> WindowState:
    """Three-valued read of one platform window: (state, types, onscreen, bounds). Never raises."""
    if not isinstance(platform_window_id, int) or isinstance(platform_window_id, bool):
        return WindowState(UNKNOWN, None, None, None)
    if platform_window_id <= 0:
        return WindowState(UNKNOWN, None, None, None)
    try:
        L = _cgs()
        info = L["cg"].CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo(
            _kCGWindowListOptionIncludingWindow, platform_window_id
        )
        if not info:
            return WindowState(UNKNOWN, None, None, None)
        try:
            cf = L["cf"]
            onscreen, bounds = None, None
            for i in range(cf.CFArrayGetCount(info)):
                row = cf.CFArrayGetValueAtIndex(info, i)
                if _cf_int(L, cf.CFDictionaryGetValue(row, L["kNumber"])) == platform_window_id:
                    onscreen, bounds = _row_facts(L, row)
                    break
        finally:
            L["cf"].CFRelease(info)
        if onscreen is None:  # no row for this window
            return WindowState(UNKNOWN, None, None, None)
        types = _space_types(L, platform_window_id)
        return WindowState(classify(types, onscreen), types, onscreen, bounds)
    except Exception:
        return WindowState(UNKNOWN, None, None, None)


def pid_window_states(pid: int) -> list[tuple[int, WindowState]] | None:
    """Every layer-0 window taller than 200 px owned by <pid> (probe.swift's filter: kitty's own
    helper windows are smaller), with its Space types. None = the window list could not be read."""
    try:
        L = _cgs()
        cf = L["cf"]
        info = L["cg"].CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo(_kCGWindowListOptionAll, 0)
        if not info:
            return None
        rows = []
        try:
            for i in range(cf.CFArrayGetCount(info)):
                row = cf.CFArrayGetValueAtIndex(info, i)
                if _cf_int(L, cf.CFDictionaryGetValue(row, L["kPid"])) != pid:
                    continue
                if _cf_int(L, cf.CFDictionaryGetValue(row, L["kLayer"])) != 0:
                    continue
                onscreen, bounds = _row_facts(L, row)
                if bounds is None or bounds[3] <= 200:
                    continue
                num = _cf_int(L, cf.CFDictionaryGetValue(row, L["kNumber"]))
                rows.append((num, onscreen, bounds))
        finally:
            cf.CFRelease(info)
        out = []
        for num, onscreen, bounds in rows:
            types = _space_types(L, num) if num else None
            out.append((num or 0, WindowState(classify(types, onscreen), types, onscreen, bounds)))
        return out
    except Exception:
        return None


# ── relax on the fix: the policy file and the per-process fsfix cache (docstring § RELAX) ────────


def state_dir() -> str:
    # HOME, not CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, for the reason lib/kitty_pane_actions.py gives: one machine, one
    # kitty, four account config dirs.
    return os.environ.get("CC_KITTY_FS_GUARD_STATE_DIR") or os.path.join(
        os.path.expanduser("~"), ".claude", "state", "kitty-fs-guard"
    )


def read_policy() -> tuple[str, str | None]:
    """→ (policy in effect, the raw value when it was refused). Absent/empty = BLOCK_STOCK; an
    unknown or unreadable value is ENFORCE, said once on stderr — the caller reads it once a run."""
    path = os.path.join(state_dir(), "policy")
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            text = fh.read(4096)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return BLOCK_STOCK, None
    except OSError as e:
        raw = f"unreadable: {e.__class__.__name__}"
    else:
        lines = [ln.strip() for ln in text.splitlines()]
        value = next((ln for ln in lines if ln and not ln.startswith("#")), "")
        if not value:
            return BLOCK_STOCK, None
        if value in POLICIES:
            return value, None
        raw = value[:80]
    print(f"{TOOL}: policy file {path}: {raw!r} is not one of {', '.join(POLICIES)} — enforcing", file=sys.stderr)
    return ENFORCE, raw


def relax_reason(policy: str, fsfix: Any, mode: str) -> str | None:
    """May a refused-fs-onscreen verdict be relaxed? → "fsfix" | "policy" | None. Decided BEFORE
    any state read, because a relaxable call must also be a read-only one (never toggles)."""
    if policy == ENFORCE or policy not in POLICIES:
        return None
    if fsfix is True:
        return "fsfix"
    if policy == RELAX_STOCK or (policy == RELAX_STOCK_REFUSE and mode in ("refuse", "any-fs")):
        return "policy"
    return None


def proc_start(pid: int) -> int | None:
    """<pid>'s start time as an epoch second, read the way scripts/kitty-build-swap.sh cmd_verify
    reads it (`ps -o lstart=`). None = no such process or unreadable — the cache then never hits."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(
            ["/bin/ps", "-o", "lstart=", "-p", str(int(pid))],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            timeout=KITTY_CALL_TIMEOUT_S,
            env=dict(os.environ, LC_ALL="C"),
        )
        text = " ".join((r.stdout or "").split())
        if r.returncode != 0 or not text:
            return None
        return int(time.mktime(time.strptime(text, "%a %b %d %H:%M:%S %Y")))
    except Exception:
        return None


def _fsfix_path(pid: int, start: int) -> str:
    return os.path.join(state_dir(), f"fsfix-{pid}-{start}")


def cache_fsfix(info: dict) -> None:
    """Remember a non-None fsfix for the kitty process that reported it. Best-effort: a guard call
    never fails on its own bookkeeping, and a cache that did not get written only means enforce."""
    pid, fsfix = info.get("pid"), info.get("fsfix")
    if not isinstance(fsfix, bool) or not isinstance(pid, int) or isinstance(pid, bool) or pid <= 0:
        return
    start = proc_start(pid)
    if start is None:
        return
    path = _fsfix_path(pid, start)
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        tmp = f"{path}.tmp{os.getpid()}"
        with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write("true\n" if fsfix else "false\n")
        os.replace(tmp, path)
        for old in glob.glob(os.path.join(state_dir(), f"fsfix-{pid}-*")):
            if old != path and ".tmp" not in os.path.basename(old):  # never a writer's in-flight tmp
                try:
                    os.unlink(old)  # an earlier process with this pid: dead by definition
                except OSError:
                    pass
    except OSError:
        pass


# ── concurrent "not last" answers: identity claims counted against the snapshot (docstring § CLAIMS) ─


def claims_dir() -> str:
    return os.environ.get("CC_KITTY_FS_GUARD_CLAIMS_DIR") or state_dir()


def _int_list(v: Any) -> list[int] | None:
    if not isinstance(v, list) or not all(isinstance(x, int) and not isinstance(x, bool) for x in v):
        return None
    return v


def claim_gate(info: dict, wid: int) -> tuple[str, int | None]:
    """→ (claim, pending) for a pane the kitten called not-last. claim is `claimed` (safe; our claim
    is written), `last` (every other pane in this snapshot is gone-or-leaving: take the last-pane
    path), `unavailable` or `error` (no reservation: HEAD semantics). pending = other live claims on
    this snapshot's panes."""
    pane_wids = _int_list(info.get("pane_wids"))
    pid = info.get("pid")
    me = info.get("pane_wid", wid)
    if (
        pane_wids is None
        or len(pane_wids) != info.get("panes_in_os_window")
        or not isinstance(pid, int)
        or isinstance(pid, bool)
        or not isinstance(me, int)
        or me not in pane_wids
    ):
        return "unavailable", None
    path = os.path.join(claims_dir(), f"claims-{pid}.json")
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT, 0o600)
        try:
            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
            raw = b""
            while chunk := os.read(fd, 65536):
                raw += chunk
            try:
                parsed = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8")) if raw.strip() else {}
            except (UnicodeDecodeError, json.JSONDecodeError):
                parsed = {}  # a torn write loses its claims; rewriting below heals the file
            now = _wall()
            live: dict[int, float] = {}
            for k, ts in (parsed.items() if isinstance(parsed, dict) else ()):
                # abs(): a claim from the future (a clock step back) also ages out instead of sticking
                if isinstance(ts, (int, float)) and abs(now - ts) < CLAIM_TTL_S and str(k).isdigit():
                    live[int(k)] = float(ts)
            others = {w for w in live if w != me}
            pending = len(others & set(pane_wids))
            remaining = set(pane_wids) - {me} - others
            claim = "claimed" if remaining else "last"
            if remaining:
                live[me] = now
            os.lseek(fd, 0, os.SEEK_SET)
            os.ftruncate(fd, 0)
            os.write(fd, json.dumps({str(k): v for k, v in live.items()}, sort_keys=True).encode())
            return claim, pending
        finally:
            os.close(fd)  # releases the flock
    except OSError:
        return "error", None


def cached_fsfix(pid: int) -> bool | None:
    """The cached fsfix for THIS process (pid + start time), or None: missing, unreadable, or written
    for an earlier process that had the same pid."""
    start = proc_start(pid)
    if start is None:
        return None
    try:
        with open(_fsfix_path(pid, start), encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            value = fh.read(16).strip()
    except OSError:
        return None
    return {"true": True, "false": False}.get(value)


# ── the decision ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


def _alert_message(wid: int, verdict: str, last: WindowState) -> str:
    """The alert says what the refusal LEFT BEHIND. The old single text ("did not leave fullscreen
    … Leave fullscreen and try again") was shown after the window had already left fullscreen and
    when no toggle was sent at all (docs/research/kitty-lastpane-move-2026-10-19/README.md § 1b);
    following it re-entered fullscreen on a window that was already windowed."""
    seen = f"(last state: {last.state}, Space types {last.types}, onscreen {last.onscreen})"
    if verdict == "refused-timeout" and last.types == [0]:
        return (
            f"kitty window {wid} left fullscreen and is now a normal window on the desktop, still "
            "holding the pane: the desktop was not on screen long enough to confirm it had settled "
            f"{seen}. Go to that window and choose Move again; it will not leave fullscreen a "
            "second time."
        )
    if verdict == "refused-timeout":
        return (
            f"kitty window {wid} was asked to leave fullscreen and did not settle within "
            f"{AFTER_DEADLINE_S:g} s {seen}. The pane was not moved. Find the window before "
            "trying again."
        )
    if verdict == "refused-entered":
        return (
            f"kitty window {wid} entered fullscreen instead of leaving it and was toggled back "
            f"{seen}. The pane was not moved."
        )
    return (
        f"Nothing changed: kitty window {wid}'s fullscreen Space was not on screen when the "
        f"request started {seen}. Go to that window and try again."
    )


def _alert(wid: int, verdict: str, last: WindowState) -> None:
    """Toggle-mode refusals only. Strings travel through argv, never into the script text, and
    nothing names application "kitty" (that LAUNCHES a dead kitty — how it came back at 00:01:28)."""
    script = [
        "-e", "on run argv",
        "-e", "display alert (item 1 of argv) message (item 2 of argv) giving up after 55",
        "-e", "end run",
    ]
    title = "kitty: pane not moved or closed"
    msg = _alert_message(wid, verdict, last)
    try:
        subprocess.run(
            ["osascript", *script, title, msg],
            capture_output=True,
            timeout=ALERT_TIMEOUT_S,
        )
    except Exception:
        pass


SAFE_VERDICTS = ("safe-windowed", "safe-windowed-offscreen")


def _is_bg_windowed(s: WindowState) -> bool:
    """Space type exactly [0] with bounds, but not on screen: a normal window on a desktop the
    display is not showing — or the 0.17-0.39 s tail of an exit animation, which is why it only
    counts after _hold_bg_windowed. classify() calls it UNKNOWN, which refused every pane whose
    windowed OS window sat on another desktop (wid 170, 2026-10-20: 1200x828, 5 identical reads in
    11 s, left as a dead pane titled "last pane of a fullscreen window")."""
    return s.types == [0] and s.onscreen is False and s.bounds is not None


def _hold_bg_windowed(pwid: Any, first: WindowState) -> tuple[str, WindowState]:
    """Safe only after BG_STABLE_S of reads that are all windowed (on screen or not) with bounds
    identical to the first. Any other read refuses as refused-unknown — never refused-fs, so the
    relax mapping (FS + onscreen ⇒ 0) can never apply to a window that moved mid-hold."""
    start, held = _now(), first.bounds
    while True:
        _sleep(SAMPLE_S)
        s = read_window_state(pwid)
        if not (_is_bg_windowed(s) or s.state == WINDOWED) or s.bounds != held:
            return "refused-unknown", s
        if _now() - start >= BG_STABLE_S:
            return "safe-windowed-offscreen", s


def _refuse_mode(pwid: Any) -> tuple[str, WindowState]:
    start = _now()
    while True:
        s = read_window_state(pwid)
        if _is_bg_windowed(s):
            return _hold_bg_windowed(pwid, s)
        if s.state != WINDOWED:
            return ("refused-fs" if s.state == FS else "refused-unknown"), s
        if _now() - start >= STABLE_S:
            return "safe-windowed", s
        _sleep(SAMPLE_S)


def _before_toggle(pwid: Any) -> tuple[str, WindowState]:
    """→ ("safe-windowed" | "safe-windowed-offscreen" | "toggle" | "refused-not-visible" |
    "refused-unknown", last read)."""
    start = _now()
    since = None
    while True:
        s = read_window_state(pwid)
        t = _now()
        if s.state == FS:
            return ("toggle" if s.onscreen else "refused-not-visible"), s
        if s.state == WINDOWED:
            since = t if since is None else since
            if t - since >= STABLE_S:
                return "safe-windowed", s
        elif _is_bg_windowed(s):
            return _hold_bg_windowed(pwid, s)
        else:
            since = None
            if t - start >= UNKNOWN_DEADLINE_S:
                return "refused-unknown", s
        _sleep(SAMPLE_S)


def _after_toggle(pwid: Any) -> tuple[str, WindowState]:
    deadline = _now() + AFTER_DEADLINE_S
    since, held = None, None
    while True:
        s = read_window_state(pwid)
        t = _now()
        if s.state == WINDOWED and s.bounds is not None:
            if since is None or s.bounds != held:
                since, held = t, s.bounds
            elif t - since >= STABLE_S:
                r = read_window_state(pwid)
                if r.state == WINDOWED and r.bounds == held:
                    return "safe-after-toggle", r
                s, since, held = r, None, None
        else:
            since, held = None, None
        if _now() >= deadline:
            return "refused-timeout", s
        _sleep(SAMPLE_S)


def _undo_entered(addr: str | None, wid: int, pwid: Any, info: dict) -> tuple[int, str, dict, WindowState]:
    """After a toggle that reported ENTERING fullscreen: FS held for STABLE_S ⇒ the enter finished,
    send exactly one undo (its boolean ignored); WINDOWED held ⇒ the toggle never took, send none;
    neither by the deadline ⇒ send none. Always a refusal."""
    deadline = _now() + AFTER_DEADLINE_S
    held, since = None, None
    while True:
        s = read_window_state(pwid)
        t = _now()
        if s.state in (FS, WINDOWED):
            if s.state != held:
                held, since = s.state, t
            elif t - since >= STABLE_S:
                if held == FS:
                    try:
                        kitten_query(addr, wid, toggle=True)
                    except (NotFound, KittenFailed):
                        pass
                return 3, "refused-entered", info, s
        else:
            held, since = None, None
        if t >= deadline:
            return 3, "refused-timeout", info, s
        _sleep(SAMPLE_S)


def relaxed_rc(rc: int, verdict: str, last: WindowState, reason: str | None) -> tuple[int, str | None]:
    """The relax mapping (docstring § RELAX): only a refused-fs verdict whose last read was FS AND
    onscreen becomes rc 0. → (rc, the reason it was relaxed or None)."""
    if reason and rc == 3 and verdict == "refused-fs" and last.state == FS and last.onscreen is True:
        return 0, reason
    return rc, None


def decide(
    addr: str | None, wid: int, mode: str, scope: str, policy: str = BLOCK_STOCK
) -> tuple[int, str, dict, WindowState, str | None]:
    """→ (rc, verdict, kitten info, last state read, relaxed reason or None). A relaxed rc is
    already mapped (3 → 0); the caller records would_rc 3 whenever the reason is not None."""
    none = WindowState(UNKNOWN, None, None, None)
    try:
        info = kitten_query(addr, wid)
    except NotFound:
        return 4, "not-found", {}, none, None
    except KittenFailed as e:
        # The reason travels in the info dict's "error" key so the verdict row and the stderr line can
        # say WHY kitty could not be read — dropped here before 2026-10-22, when pane 42's unreadable
        # read surfaced as "last pane of a fullscreen window" (husk-panes-2026-10-22 RC5, F4).
        return 3, "kitten-failed", {"error": str(e)}, none, None
    cache_fsfix(info)
    last_for = info.get("last_for") or {}
    if last_for.get(scope) is False:
        if scope != "pane":
            return 0, "safe-not-last", info, none, None
        claim, pending = claim_gate(info, wid)
        info = dict(info, claim=claim, pending=pending)
        if claim != "last":
            return 0, "safe-not-last", info, none, None
        # every other pane of this snapshot is gone or claimed by a sibling's close: last-pane path
    elif last_for.get(scope) is not True:
        return 3, "kitten-failed", dict(info, error=f"kitten reply has no last_for[{scope}]"), none, None
    pwid = info.get("platform_window_id")

    reason = relax_reason(policy, info.get("fsfix"), mode)
    if reason is not None:
        # Relaxable ⇒ READ-ONLY, and toggle mode is COERCED TO REFUSE MODE (the brief's A2.4): both
        # run _refuse_mode, never _before_toggle. Not _before_toggle's read-only half, because its
        # UNKNOWN_DEADLINE_S grace would let an UNKNOWN read (usually a transition) that then reads
        # FS-onscreen be relaxed with no stability hold, where refuse mode refuses on the first
        # UNKNOWN (adversarial review 2026-10-16). The cost, fail-closed: on the patched build a
        # toggle-mode chord pressed mid-transition does nothing (press again) instead of waiting.
        # Toggle mode's verdict vocabulary is kept for FS offscreen: refused-not-visible, rc 3.
        verdict, s = _refuse_mode(pwid)
        if mode != "refuse" and verdict == "refused-fs" and s.onscreen is not True:
            verdict = "refused-not-visible"
        rc, relaxed = relaxed_rc(0 if verdict in SAFE_VERDICTS else 3, verdict, s, reason)
        return rc, verdict, info, s, relaxed

    if mode == "refuse":
        verdict, s = _refuse_mode(pwid)
        return (0 if verdict in SAFE_VERDICTS else 3), verdict, info, s, None

    verdict, s = _before_toggle(pwid)
    if verdict != "toggle":
        return (0 if verdict in SAFE_VERDICTS else 3), verdict, info, s, None
    try:
        toggled = kitten_query(addr, wid, toggle=True)
    except NotFound:
        return 4, "not-found", info, s, None
    except KittenFailed as e:
        return 3, "kitten-failed", dict(info, error=str(e)), s, None
    entered = toggled.get("toggled_into_fullscreen")
    if entered is True:
        # It ENTERED fullscreen: our read said FS but kitty disagreed. The undo must NOT be sent
        # now: kitty drops a toggle while `in_fullscreen_transition` is set (cocoa_window.m:2339),
        # and the boolean is computed from the styleMask even for a dropped toggle (:3686-3690). So
        # wait for the transition to END, then undo at most once — or not at all if it never took.
        return (*_undo_entered(addr, wid, pwid, info), None)
    if entered is not False:
        return 3, "kitten-failed", dict(info, error=f"toggle reply unusable: {entered!r}"), s, None
    verdict, s = _after_toggle(pwid)
    return (0 if verdict == "safe-after-toggle" else 3), verdict, info, s, None


def run_then(addr: str | None, wid: int, action: list[str]) -> int:
    """Run the native action through the kitten's `act`, in ONE kitty main-loop call that first
    re-checks <wid> exists and is still the active window. Never `kitty @ action --self` here:
    after the guard's wait, --self falls back to the ACTIVE window when <wid> is gone, and bare
    detach_window moves the active window whatever --self names (lib/kitty-fs-kitten.py act()).
    → 0 acted · 4 the window is gone · 3 focus moved off it (nothing done) · 1 the action failed."""
    cmd = _at(addr) + ["kitten", "--match", f"id:{wid}", KITTEN, "act", *action]
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=KITTY_CALL_TIMEOUT_S * 2)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        print(f"{TOOL}: --then {' '.join(action)} failed: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if r.returncode != 0 and "No matching windows" in (r.stderr or "") + (r.stdout or ""):
        print(f"{TOOL}: --then {' '.join(action)}: window {wid} is gone — nothing done", file=sys.stderr)
        return 4
    try:
        out = json.loads(r.stdout) if r.returncode == 0 else {"error": (r.stderr or "").strip()[:200]}
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, TypeError):
        out = {"error": f"unreadable act reply {(r.stdout or '')[:200]!r}"}
    if out.get("acted"):
        return 0
    err = str(out.get("error") or "not consumed")
    print(f"{TOOL}: --then {' '.join(action)}: {err} — nothing done", file=sys.stderr)
    if "gone" in err:
        return 4
    return 3 if err == "focus-moved" else 1


def _all_fs_onscreen(rows: list[tuple[int, WindowState]] | None) -> bool:
    """A readable sample in which every FS window is onscreen and no other window's read failed."""
    if rows is None:
        return False
    return all(
        s.onscreen is True if s.state == FS else s.types is not None for _n, s in rows
    )


def any_fs(pid: int, policy: str | None = None) -> int:
    """No window of <pid> reads FS on ANY sample across STABLE_S (one sample could land inside an
    enter animation, where the type never reads 4), and no read fails.

    Relaxed (docstring § RELAX) only from the per-process fsfix CACHE — remote control is down here,
    so the kitten cannot be asked — or a stock-relaxing policy, and only when every FS window reads
    onscreen and nothing was unreadable: the caller (cc-pane-close's SIGHUP fallback) does not know
    which window its pane is in, so "the target is onscreen" is provable only as "every candidate
    is". On the one-fullscreen-window-per-Desktop layout that is rarely true, and it stays refused."""
    if policy is None:
        policy, _raw = read_policy()
    start, rows = _now(), []
    while True:
        sample = pid_window_states(pid)
        if sample is None or any(s.state == FS for _n, s in sample):
            rows = sample
            break
        rows = sample
        if _now() - start >= STABLE_S:
            break
        _sleep(SAMPLE_S)
    if rows is None:
        verdict, rc, detail = "refused-unknown", 3, "window list unreadable"
    else:
        fs = [n for n, s in rows if s.state == FS]
        # A FAILED read (types None) refuses. An EMPTY Space list does not: a window on no Space
        # (kitty keeps an ordered-out 500x500 helper window) cannot be in fullscreen, and counting it
        # would make this refuse on every kitty, forever.
        bad = [n for n, s in rows if s.state != FS and s.types is None]
        if fs:
            verdict, rc, detail = "refused-fs", 3, f"fullscreen windows {fs}"
        elif bad:
            verdict, rc, detail = "refused-unknown", 3, f"unreadable windows {bad}"
        else:
            verdict, rc, detail = "safe-windowed", 0, f"{len(rows)} windows, none fullscreen"
    fsfix = cached_fsfix(pid) if policy != ENFORCE else None
    relaxed = None
    reason = relax_reason(policy, fsfix, "any-fs") if verdict == "refused-fs" else None
    if reason and _all_fs_onscreen(rows):
        # The sampling above stops at the FIRST fullscreen sample. Relaxing on it would drop the
        # "one sample is not enough" rule, so the relax holds only if every further sample up to
        # STABLE_S is also readable with every fullscreen window onscreen.
        ok = True
        while ok and _now() - start < STABLE_S:
            _sleep(SAMPLE_S)
            ok = _all_fs_onscreen(pid_window_states(pid))
        if ok:
            rc, relaxed = 0, reason
    log_row(
        TOOL,
        "verdict",
        mode="any-fs",
        verdict=verdict,
        rc=rc,
        detail=detail,
        kitty_pid=pid,
        fsfix=fsfix,
        policy=policy,
        would_rc=3 if relaxed else None,
        relaxed=relaxed,
        enforced=False if relaxed else None,
    )
    if rc:
        print(f"{TOOL}: refused ({verdict}) kitty pid {pid}: {detail}", file=sys.stderr)
    return rc


USAGE = (
    "usage: kitty-fullscreen-guard --window <id> [--to <address>] --mode toggle|refuse\n"
    "                              [--scope pane|tab|os-window] [--tool <name>] [--then <action> ...]\n"
    "       kitty-fullscreen-guard --any-fs --kitty-pid <pid>"
)


def parse(argv: list[str]) -> dict[str, Any] | None:
    opts: dict[str, Any] = {"scope": "pane", "then": None, "to": None, "tool": None, "mode": None}
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a == "--then":
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                return None
            opts["then"] = argv[i + 1 :]
            break
        if a == "--any-fs":
            opts["any_fs"] = True
            i += 1
            continue
        if a in ("--window", "--to", "--mode", "--scope", "--tool", "--kitty-pid"):
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                return None
            opts[a[2:].replace("-", "_")] = argv[i + 1]
            i += 2
            continue
        return None
    if opts.get("any_fs"):
        try:
            opts["kitty_pid"] = int(opts.get("kitty_pid") or "")
        except ValueError:
            return None
        return opts if opts["kitty_pid"] > 0 else None
    try:
        opts["window"] = int(opts.get("window") or "")
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if opts["window"] <= 0 or opts["mode"] not in ("toggle", "refuse") or opts["scope"] not in SCOPES:
        return None
    return opts


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    opts = parse(sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else argv)
    if opts is None:
        print(USAGE, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    policy, policy_raw = read_policy()  # once a run: an unknown value is said on stderr once
    if opts.get("any_fs"):
        return any_fs(opts["kitty_pid"], policy)

    wid, mode, scope = opts["window"], opts["mode"], opts["scope"]
    addr = opts["to"] or socket_address()
    rc, verdict, info, last, relaxed = decide(addr, wid, mode, scope, policy)
    log_row(
        TOOL,
        "verdict",
        wid=wid,
        os_window_id=info.get("os_window_id"),
        platform_window_id=info.get("platform_window_id"),
        panes=info.get("panes_in_os_window"),
        state=last.state if verdict != "safe-not-last" else None,
        # The read the verdict was decided on. Without these a refused-timeout could not be told
        # apart (bounds churn vs an offscreen desktop vs a Space bounce) after the fact.
        types=last.types if verdict != "safe-not-last" else None,
        onscreen=last.onscreen if verdict != "safe-not-last" else None,
        bounds=list(last.bounds) if verdict != "safe-not-last" and last.bounds else None,
        verdict=verdict,
        rc=rc,
        mode=mode,
        scope=scope,
        caller=opts["tool"],
        fsfix=info.get("fsfix"),
        policy=policy,
        policy_refused=policy_raw,
        would_rc=3 if relaxed else None,
        relaxed=relaxed,
        enforced=False if relaxed else None,
        error=info.get("error"),
        # docstring § CLAIMS: n/a when no claim gate ran (another scope, or the kitten said last)
        claim=info.get("claim", "n/a"),
        pending=info.get("pending"),
    )
    if rc != 0:
        if verdict == "kitten-failed":
            # No window state was read, so a state line here would print placeholders
            # (state=UNKNOWN types=None) that read as a measurement. Say what failed instead.
            print(
                f"{TOOL}: refused (kitten-failed) could not read kitty window {wid}: "
                f"{info.get('error') or 'no reason recorded'}",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
        else:
            print(
                f"{TOOL}: refused ({verdict}) kitty window {wid} scope={scope} mode={mode}: "
                f"state={last.state} types={last.types} onscreen={last.onscreen}",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
        if rc == 3 and mode == "toggle":
            _alert(wid, verdict, last)
        return rc
    if opts["then"]:
        trc = run_then(addr, wid, opts["then"])
        log_row(TOOL, "result", wid=wid, choice=" ".join(opts["then"]), rc=trc, caller=opts["tool"])
        return trc
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
